eated  which tend to enrich a few people very  much indeed and create such change in  disruption for others that they become  impoverished we've seen it in all our  industrial revolutions but I think that  if we assert ourselves as a democracy  and as a state and as a polity as a  people as a society we can actually make  sure that we ensure that the benefits of  these changes and disruptions that are  coming our way very very rapidly and at  an increasing rate can actually be  shared more widely and that we can get  through this time of disjunction with a  lot more fairness and equity than we  would in the absence of that kind of  ambition to manage it so let's go back  to the paradox of our labor market  because whenever our current government  and I'm not being party political  obviously you wouldn't expect me to be  that way at an event like this whenever  it wants to boast about something it  boast about how fantastically successful  the labor market is and it always talks  in quantitative terms about how many  jobs have been created how we have the  largest number of people in employment  ever in our history partially because  our populations increase but that's  that's another issue and large amounts  of new jobs are being created it's all  very dynamic and wonderful but at the  same time the satisfaction levels for  some employees are so high and this job  satisfaction and yet at the same time  and alongside the mothers are struggling  to make ends meet and they're never  being able to  escape in work poverty and insecurity so  we've got this paradox going on which  you can resolve I think if you've looked  behind some of the headline figures  about quantity and start looking about  quality of the sorts of jobs that are on  offer so we have to consider what is  happening to quality in the job market  rather than just thinking about numbers  of jobs and taking this perspective it  actually becomes clear that there's an  increasing segmentation in our labor  market into what what I've being  characterized as lovely and lousy jobs  and if you're in the lovely sector  you've got your own pension you know  what your hours are you're probably  progressing you know that you can be  promoted you're shoring up your pension  you've got a holiday paying sick pay  time off agency at work and all of those  things and you're having great time and  it's very fulfilling you feel as a human  being that you're that you're helping  provide a service in a collective way  whatever it is you're doing and you're  enjoying your job that's the lovely end  and then at the lousy end this is very  very different the lousy end tends to be  jobs which are on minimum wage it tends  to be jobs where the hours aren't  guaranteed and can go down as well as up  also quite often where there's a great  deal of insecurity and a genuine no  knowledge whatsoever about the hours  that you'll be guaranteed in the future  but your costs stay the same quite often  it might be a zero hours contract it  might be a part-time work contract or an  agency contract and so you can't  guarantee your hours going forwards and  yet your outgoings are all too  predictably the same at the same time  the pay rises aren't happening and your  pay has probably been stagnant for at  least ten years and you're not getting  real terms pay increases but say  time if you're in the private rented  sector rents are going up costs are  going up the cost of utilities are going  up so you are actually in the situation  where you're working but you're not  making any progress and you can't  actually see an end to that kind of  treadmill that you're on employers quite  often won't say their employers and now  make you contract separately and if you  do that if you become apparently  self-employed or you incorporate that  can be a good choice if you're a  freelancer who can command a huge price  but all too often it's done as a sort of  scam to ensure that your employer  doesn't have to your 